cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "Post-quantum certificate chain budget: per-site projection against the initial congestion window"
message: >-
  If you use this corpus or cite its figures, please cite it as below. Read
  PRIOR-ART.md first. It records, per claim, what published work already covers,
  and several figures here are refinements of prior results rather than new ones.
  Every post-quantum figure is stamped Proposed, permanently: the input chains are
  measured, the migrated sizes are projected under stated assumptions.
type: software
authors:
  - given-names: "Danny B."
    family-names: Carr
    name-suffix: Jr.
    affiliation: "Carr Digital LLC"
repository-code: "https://github.com/DannyBCarrJr/pqc-chain-budget"
url: "https://carrdigital.dev/writing/the-typical-chain-moved/"
doi: 10.5281/zenodo.21846142
identifiers:
  - type: doi
    value: 10.5281/zenodo.21846142
    description: >-
      Concept DOI. Always resolves to the latest version; cite this one unless you
      need to pin a specific release.
  - type: doi
    value: 10.5281/zenodo.21846143
    description: Version DOI for v1.0.0.
abstract: >-
  Post-quantum signature sizes projected onto each site's own deployed certificate
  chain, rather than onto a single constructed typical chain. Input chains are
  Verified: captured 2026-08-07 from the Tranco list ZJGPG, where 8,152 of the top
  10,000 domains completed a TLS handshake and 8,151 were fully parseable. One chain
  is dropped from the projection because a certificate's signature or subject public
  key did not parse, on the principle that projecting part of a chain is worse than
  dropping it.

  Under a drop-in ML-DSA-44 migration, with the full chain migrated and SCTs left
  classical, 85.1% of measured sites project past the IW10 initial congestion window,
  which costs an extra round trip on a full handshake. A leaf-only migration fits
  almost everywhere: 0.3% exceed IW10 for ML-DSA-44 and 0.9% for ML-DSA-65. The
  direction was already predicted by typical-chain models; what this corpus adds is
  the fraction, the spread, and the per-site verdicts.

  Certificate compression recovers a median 985 bytes, 28.6%, on these chains today
  and roughly the same 985 bytes after migration, which is 7.4% of an ML-DSA-44 chain
  and 5.5% of an ML-DSA-65 one. The saving is structural and migration adds no
  structure. That compression does not rescue post-quantum certificates is stated in
  two IETF drafts and is cited here rather than claimed, and measuring compression
  across a large corpus of real chains is likewise published work.

  Method note: the classical half of this approach is Nawrocki et al. (CoNEXT 2022),
  who evaluated real deployed chains per site against a first-flight transport budget.
  This work is their method with a post-quantum signature substitution.
keywords:
  - post-quantum cryptography
  - TLS
  - X.509
  - public key infrastructure
  - ML-DSA
  - certificate chain size
  - congestion window
  - certificate compression
  - measurement
license: MIT
version: 1.0.0
date-released: "2026-08-09"

# Added 2026-08-12, after the v1.0.0 release rather than before it, which had a
# visible cost worth recording: with no citation file present at archive time, Zenodo
# fell back to the GitHub-generated string "DannyBCarrJr/pqc-chain-budget: v1.0.0" as
# the record title. That is poor for search and for Google Scholar. Zenodo metadata is
# editable after publication, so the existing record's title should be corrected there
# by hand; this file fixes it for every future release.
#
# Both DOIs were read from DataCite, not inferred: the concept DOI reports HasVersion
# -> ...21846143 and the version DOI reports IsVersionOf -> ...21846142, so the pair is
# confirmed from both directions.
